The original does a linear scan over the whole message table on every call. This version builds a 64K-entry direct lookup table once, on the first call, from the constant `mesgs` profile array. After that, every lookup is a single array index.

- **Same results:** the table is filled only for slots that are still empty. When `num` values repeat, the lowest index wins, just as the original scan returned the first match.
- **Missing numbers:** a number with no entry still returns `NULL`.
- **Thread safety:** initialization of the function-local static is thread-safe in C++11 and later.
- **No mutable-state cache:** the table depends only on the constant profile data.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct LookupTable
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        LookupTable()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                if (idx[n] == 0xFFFF)
                    idx[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
            }
        }
    };

    static const LookupTable table;

    FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
    if (i == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[i];
}
```